92186115458 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cf42fdc1-dee5-53a6-abe0-79ec4ef7b900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116351 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116351 #edb/ref 1061 615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116351 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62b549c4-9fa6-595c-9705-edecd57fab60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116351 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116352 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116352 #edb/ref 1061 616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116352 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7d001457-3a19-5fb6-b582-e4447e917a71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116352 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116353 #edb/ref 1061 619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116353 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3a114b6-40b8-5189-a0c2-30250448ba57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116353 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116354 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116354 #edb/ref 1061 622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116354 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "872a7f94-c8a5-5f55-a91f-39909aca384f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116354 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116355 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116355 #edb/ref 1061 623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116355 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4284ae9c-1207-5fcc-829e-569528302322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116355 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116507 #edb/ref 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **differentiable** at a point $x=a$ if the derivative $f'(a)$ exists.\n\nIn general:\n\n- If a function is not continuous at some point, then it is not differentiable at that point either.\n\n- If a function is differentiable at some point, then it must be continuous at that point.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given a step function $y=f(x)$ that has a jump discontinuity at $x=3,$ given below. We will verify that $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3$ by showing that $f'(3)$ does not exist. \n\n![](images/9f/9f91b034a27ce7fd2b432f0b094e4b40098247916b78f9c1b3eb64c7214f69b4.png)\n\nThe value $f'(3)$ is a limit, so to check whether it exists, we need to compute the left and right-sided limits.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, we have that $f(x) = 2.$ So the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2-2}{x-3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, we have that $f(x)=1.$ So the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1-2}{x-3}={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{-1}{x-3}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}=\\text{DNE}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116507 #edb/ref 1065 332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116507 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60845968-f9d5-58f2-9205-7c60bc949ed6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116507 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116514 #edb/ref 1064 "If a function is continuous, that does *not* mean that it's differentiable!\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $f(x)=|x|.$ The function $f(x)$ is continuous everywhere, including the point $x=0.$ But is $f(x)$ also differentiable everywhere? \n\n![](images/18/18644551713e0c1d451cf27bd43f3cc8fc8d7064d7af79df3cd2070af782c25e.png)\n\nFor $x < 0,$ the graph consists of a downwards-sloping line with slope $-1.$ For $x > 0,$ the graph consists of an upward-sloping line with slope $1.$ So, the derivative of the absolute value function is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-1\\, & x<0, \\\\ 1\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the derivative of $f(x)=|x|$ does not exist at $x=0.$ This is because the limit of the slope as we approach $x=0$ from the left is not equal to the limit as we approach from the right.\n\nIn general, a continuous function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=a$ if the function $f(x)$ has any of the following features:\n\n- A **corner point** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x)=|x|$ at $x=0.$\n\n- A **cusp** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x) = \\sqrt{|x|}$ at $x=0.$\n\n- A **vertical tangent** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at $x=0.$\n\n![](images/28/28a6f3a424556e1e60e4959b03a5c38a1ff6a5d0efb110a0e5a4bfadc7072511.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116514 #edb/ref 1065 335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116514 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0df8723a-c6d6-562d-8cde-b44a47dd70bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116514 #edb/ref 1067 "Continuity Does Not Imply Differentiability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330010 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330010 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330010 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06e68ed1-6c21-5339-b817-0dca6bed6e25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330114 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330114 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330114 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13dd78f4-7836-5e2c-95cf-18fff06f843d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330439 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330439 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330439 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "36a4f226-546a-58fe-9e0a-5f6172d50d49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331207 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331207 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331207 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8dbffc66-920f-5159-966d-348e80294b49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332194 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332194 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332194 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fea5d391-e5d1-5006-b2d7-39671ae5c2c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332568 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332568 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332568 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332568 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332568 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186332194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332568 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332568 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332568 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186332194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332568 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d1ab9f03-8ca5-52dc-a6f7-f2d81ee5a0b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332843 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51067" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332843 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332843 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$, has a cusp at $x=1,$ and is differentiable at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\nI. It is differentiable at $x=-2$\n\nII. It's **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332843 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "227ca0fe-b4ce-570a-82ea-2de7f8f95c49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332843 #edb/ref 1074 "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x+2|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a cusp at $x=1$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
